    "q-251801": {
      "dom_id": "step-q251801",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Consider the two sets of $6$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $1$ | $7$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $11$ | $3$ | $7$ | $2$ | $1$ | $18$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=247,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+7+3+4+6+10=31, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =11+3+7+2+1+18=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=247.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=247-\\frac{1}{6}(31)(42)=247-217=30.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
