    "q-251493": {
      "dom_id": "step-q251493",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the two sets of $6$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $3$ | $9$ | $7$ | $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $16$ | $15$ | $14$ | $23$ | $18$ | $15$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=514,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2+3+9+7+5+4=30, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =16+15+14+23+18+15=101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=514.$\n\nFinally, substituting in the above formula we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=514-\\frac{1}{6}(30)(101)=514-505=9.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
